Go.dot patch 0004 for JUCE: unzipping cannot write outside its folder or past an entry's declared size.

JUCE's own fixes, in 8.0.17-security and 9.0.5, cherry-picked from JUCE's develop:
  d670f9a09734 "ZipFile: Confine symbolic links to the target directory and
               reject oversized entries" (31b3b680639c on develop, Tom Poole, 2026-10-20)
  a5c72f1ed8c4 "ZipFile: Check for a symbolic parent before deleting an existing
               target file" (2303b551cf51 on develop, Tom Poole, 2026-11-02)
Each develop commit applies cleanly to this pin (9.0.4+26, which they follow),
with Go.dot's notice after JUCE's licence block.

The faults: an archive could hold a symbolic link pointing anywhere on the
machine, and a later entry written through it landed there; an entry could hold
more than the size its central directory declares, filling the disk; and an
entry overwriting a file through a link already in the target folder deleted
the file the link pointed at. Go.dot
unzips Pd patch archives (PatchEditor.cpp) and the ffmpeg it downloads
(FfmpegInstall.cpp).

What changes for Go.dot: nothing for a well-formed archive. An archive with a
link leading out of the target folder, or an understated entry, stops
extracting at that entry and says so.

LINE ENDINGS. JUCE stores these files with CRLF in its own repository, so every
line of the hunks below ends in CRLF and must stay so; .gitattributes keeps this
file byte for byte (-text), and scripts/patches.py writes it as bytes.

Applied by Go.dot's build, cmake/WfgJucePatches.cmake, in the order
patches/JUCE/series gives; refreshed from the working tree with
scripts/patches.py JUCE refresh, which keeps this header. Against JUCE
7ff644f65d7d (9.0.4+26, the JUCE Tracktion Engine a5e44996f5f pins), where the
fixes are JUCE develop's own commits, cherry-picked. Drop it when the pin moves
to 9.0.5 or later. The author's decision of 2026-11-02.

